WEBVTT

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:12.870
Starring Barbara Billingsley, Hugh Beaumont,

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:29.760
Tony Dowell, and Tony Mathers as The Beaver.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.420
- Are you in a good mood?

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.480
- Huh?

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:43.100
- Well, Beaver wants me to show you something,

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.100
but only if you're in a good mood,

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.440
not if you're in a bad mood.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:49.900
- Well, I had a miserable day at the office

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:51.670
and a charming dinner.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:58.090
So I guess I can go either way.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:00:59.220
Football hat,

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.640
A sweatshirt with numbers on it and football pants.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.750
Oh, he said he only wanted the pants if the other fellas get them.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:12.130
Beaver and his friends have formed a football team called the Lightning Eleven and he's the captain.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:14.160
Oh, the captain, huh?

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.780
Well, I think the captain ought to have pants even if the other fellas don't.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.880
I'll go up in a little bit and have a talk with the old pro.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:26.590
Me for quarterback and Gilbert for fullback 'cause he's the biggest guy.

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:29.730
He won't play unless we put him in the backfield.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.280
Hey, Beaver, that's only six guys.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.120
How can you call six guys the Lightning Eleven?

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:38.380
Who ever heard of a football team called the Lightning Six?

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:42.460
Anyway, the Lightning Eleven is the kind of name that scares guys.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:44.600
Hey, who are you guys going to play?

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.640
Well, the Grand Avenue Tigers, as soon as they get two more guys.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.600
Hey, where'd they get that name, Tigers?

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:52.350
Rick Rickover is the captain.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.610
He's got a shirt with a tiger on it.

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:56.170
On account his father went to Princeton.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:57.660
That's a school.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:01:59.640
Hey, B.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.820
You want that Eddie Haskell and I should coach your team for you?

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.740
It'd be neat if you did, Wally.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.420
But, hey, he's a mean guy.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.280
Well, I'll do most of the coaching.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:11.100
Eddie will just kind of hang around.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:13.720
But he's the kind of guy that even hangs around mean.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.140
I won't let him hang around mean.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.480
Then I guess it'd be okay.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:27.310
Well, Beaver, I, uh, I see you want a little football equipment.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:28.470
Yeah, Dad.

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.000
You think I could please get some shoes?

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.870
shoulder pads and a helmet with rubber in it so I can bang my head all I want?

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:38.620
Well, I think we can get you a helmet, Beaver, but isn't there something of Wally's around

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:39.520
here you can use?

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:40.540
Well, sure.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.050
He can use my old football pants, ones I used to have when I was a kid.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:45.620
Are they clean or dirty?

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:47.380
I think they're kind of dirty.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.570
Well, if they're dirty, I'll wear them, 'cause I don't want to look brand new all over.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:57.970
You know, Beaver, when I was a boy, we didn't have a lot of fancy equipment.

00:02:57.970 --> 00:03:00.000
Matter of fact, to make a helmet, my brother

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.880
and I used to take an old cap and stuff it with newspaper.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.220
- Why, I'd sure hate to run into a goalpost

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:07.740
for the hat full of newspaper.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:10.560
- Yeah, and then we'd take cardboard

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.790
and put it in our knickers for knee pads.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:15.610
- And on the way home, did rich ladies give you free soup?

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:16.610
- What'd you ask that?

00:03:16.610 --> 00:03:18.230
- Well, I saw a movie once

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:20.030
where this poor kid was walking down the street

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:21.590
and rich ladies gave him free soup.

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:26.660
- Boy, you sure must have had a funny looking team, Dad.

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:28.350
- Well, maybe we were, Wally,

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:30.000
but if we managed to enjoy ourselves,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.720
without a lot of fancy equipment.

00:03:31.720 --> 00:03:34.490
- Does this mean you're not gonna buy me this stuff, Dad?

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:35.780
- No, Beaver.

00:03:35.780 --> 00:03:39.090
I think I can get you a helmet and a sweatshirt.

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.270
I guess today a kid can't even play marbles

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.000
without a uniform.

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.210
- Thanks a lot, Dad.

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:48.360
- Say, Beaver, about this football team.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.250
This is the baseball season, isn't it?

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.780
- Well, sure, Dad, but he's just a kid.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:58.730
What does he care what season that is?

00:03:58.730 --> 00:03:59.480
- Oh, are you in here?

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:01.140
Yeah, I'm in here

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.450
I thought you took the boys over to Metzger's Field

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:06.920
Oh, I did, but I've been back about 15 minutes

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.950
How's the football team coming?

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.980
Well, I didn't stick around very long

00:04:11.980 --> 00:04:13.900
All the kids gave me that look

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:14.930
What look?

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:16.790
You know, like I was a father

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:22.240
Oh, there's nothing like a Saturday at home

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.050
No running down to the office

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:26.750
No phone calls, no teletypes

00:04:26.750 --> 00:04:28.800
No people running in to bother you

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.000
That's nice, dear

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:33.080
But there are a few things I'd like you to do for me.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:34.790
Oh?

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.790
The window in the kitchen sticks, the bulbs out in the basement,

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:40.290
there's some kind of funny-looking bugs all over the roses,

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.330
and the vacuum cleaner doesn't work.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.670
Oh, and dear, would you run down to the market and get me some wax?

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.770
I want to do the kitchen floor.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:54.520
I just can't wait till Monday to get back to that office and take it easy.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:14.090
Let's go.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:16.490
Whitey, don't you stand over the ball.

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:17.820
Crouch down over it.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:22.020
I'm crouched down up here, but the rest of me won't crouch.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:23.240
Come on, let's go.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.040
Let's get this show on the road.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:27.000
OK, you guys, line up.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Come on, snap into it, you clods.

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:40.760
it out, will you, Eddie? Is it my fault the kid can't get out of his own way? Come on,

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:46.990
let's go, babe. Uh, we're gonna go out for a few passes now. Okay, Gilbert, you go out

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:58.140
and cut to the right. One, two, three, hike. Gilbert, I said cut to the right. How come

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.000
you went left? I didn't know.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:05.260
- Both the same.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.140
- Let me show these owls how to do it.

00:06:21.740 --> 00:06:23.940
- Oh, everything up until he fell down.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.860
- Okay, you guys, let's get under these puns.

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:37.680
- One, two, three, hike.

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.560
Hey, Coach, I think you got him over-trained.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:48.450
Okay, you guys, come on back.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:50.670
- Coach, can I be excused?

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:53.430
I gotta go home and take my cod liver oil.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:56.210
- Well, I was just gonna give you some plays.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:58.210
Are you gonna let the team down?

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:00.000
- My father says if I don't take it,

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:02.700
my cod liver oil, my teeth will flow out.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:05.060
Look, kid, what do you want to be?

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:07.120
All American or have teeth?

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.560
Okay, I'll stay.

00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:13.320
Okay, everybody over here.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:17.050
Okay, now, Beaver, you take the ball from Whitey,

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:18.630
and then you pretend to give it to Gilbert

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:20.290
when he comes through the center of the line.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:21.260
Now watch.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:23.380
One, two, three, hike.

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:27.350
And then you pretend to watch the whole play,

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:30.000
and then after everybody's sucked in,

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.500
You take the ball, and you run around the end,

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.570
and you make a touchdown all by yourself.

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:35.800
- Hey, Wally, that's neat.

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:40.730
When am I gonna get to make a touchdown all by myself?

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.150
- Later, Gilbert, later.

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:43.570
Now, look, you guys try it.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:45.780
Don't goof up anything, Beef.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:46.860
- Signal's set.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.160
One, two, three, hike.

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:54.230
- Kid, what are you doing?

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.390
Playing football or looking for Indians?

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:58.610
Come on, run, Beef.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:10.480
okay okay come on back that's gonna be our secret play we'll call it old 98

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:19.570
come on hurry up boy Wally these pants of yours are the slowest pants I ever ran in

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:30.000
down in the basement putting in a new bulb oh did you look at those funny-looking bugs on

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:36.170
the roses uh-huh what do you think i think the funniest looking bugs i ever saw uh where's the

00:08:36.170 --> 00:08:45.540
vacuum cleaner you want it fixed it's in the hall closet hello woolly hi mom it's lunch ready yet

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:50.340
we'll be in a few minutes oh well would it be okay if i asked eddie haskell to stay over for lunch

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:56.790
today well i don't know do you want to go to all the trouble of calling him oh it's no trouble mom

00:09:00.090 --> 00:09:03.690
- Hey, Eddie.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:05.050
- It's okay, Eddie.

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:06.150
- There, you see, Sam?

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:08.200
I told you'd work.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:10.890
- Eddie?

00:09:10.890 --> 00:09:12.140
- Mrs. Cleaver.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.980
Wally and I were just discussing our football practice.

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:17.200
One of the plays works very well.

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.400
- Yes, doesn't it?

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.450
- I'm gonna need a screwdriver.

00:09:21.450 --> 00:09:22.490
- Oh, hello, fellas.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:23.310
- Hi, Dan.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:24.910
- How do you do, Mr. Cleaver?

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:27.130
I've been invited to stay for lunch.

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:28.420
- Oh, fine.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:29.670
How's the team coming along?

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:31.520
I believe the squad is shaping up.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.650
Yeah, some of those guys are kind of okay.

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:35.340
Well, that's fine.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:36.260
Maybe after a little bit,

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.000
I could come over and give them a few pointers.

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:39.760
I played a little football myself.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.760
Yes, Beaver was telling all the fellas on the field

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.720
how you used to stuff newspapers in your cab.

00:09:45.620 --> 00:09:48.660
I meant later on, Eddie.

00:09:49.430 --> 00:09:50.810
Of course, Mr. Cleaver.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:52.260
Where is the Beaver?

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:54.210
Oh, he and some of the guys

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:55.530
stopped off at the soda fountain

00:09:55.530 --> 00:09:56.550
to kind of show off.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:57.370
He'll be along.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:00.000
Thank you very much for having me

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.230
lunch mrs cleaver i hope it's no trouble oh no eddie we're just having chopped egg sandwiches

00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:15.750
that would be delicious boy wally chopped egg sandwiches can't you talk your mother into

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:20.710
something with a little meat in it right down will you eddie okay okay

00:10:39.690 --> 00:11:00.000
Penny. I saw you playing football. I saw you seeing me. These aren't my pants. You got a good team, Beaver. Oh, it's okay. We're going to play the Grand Avenue Tigers. Are you going to beat them? Sure. But your brother's on our team. Why don't you ask him? When I

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.460
- Ask 'em stuff, he hits me.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:03.900
- That's 'cause you're a sister.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.780
- Yeah.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.820
- Most of the tigers are bigger than you fellows.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:11.540
- Yeah, but we got a secret play.

00:11:11.540 --> 00:11:13.250
- What's a secret play?

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:14.890
- That's a play that nobody knows about

00:11:14.890 --> 00:11:16.680
that always wins the game.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:18.180
- Is it a good secret play?

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.770
- Sure, my brother made it up.

00:11:19.770 --> 00:11:21.070
It's called the '098.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:23.970
Here, hold it.

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:33.270
I get the ball from White, and I pretend I had it to Gilbert.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:39.490
Then I sneak back, and when nobody's looking, I run around the end and make a touchdown.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:42.370
Gee, your brother must be pretty smart.

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:47.710
Yeah, he's the one that made it up, but I'm the one that has to do it.

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:49.380
Well, I gotta go home now.

00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:53.950
It's okay, Penny. You can have the rest of it.

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:55.130
I'll see you.

00:12:00.030 --> 00:12:03.160
Who's here?

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:04.080
Penny.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:05.220
Hiya, Penny.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:07.320
Where's your dumb brother?

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:09.330
He's not a dumb brother.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:11.240
He plays on the Lightning Eleven.

00:12:11.240 --> 00:12:13.210
He's gonna beat you, dopey tigers.

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:15.210
Ah, go on. We'll slaughter 'em.

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:17.030
No, you won't.

00:12:17.030 --> 00:12:19.140
Sure we will.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:21.140
No, you won't, because they've got a secret play.

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:24.880
And Beaver's gonna make a touchdown when nobody's looking.

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:28.160
What do you mean, a secret play?

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:30.000
Well, Beaver pretends

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:36.380
he hands the ball to Gilbert, but instead, after everyone's fooled, he makes a touchdown.

00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:38.020
Oh, yeah?

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:46.190
Yeah, and it's called old 98. You just wait and see what happens.

00:12:46.190 --> 00:12:51.690
How do you like that, Richard? She told us the whole secret play.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:55.250
Yeah, she's even dumber than her dumb brother.

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:07.700
dear do you think this saturday you could do something about those funny looking bugs on the

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:13.570
roses oh sure uh i think i'll call someone over at the nursery this afternoon have them come over

00:13:13.570 --> 00:13:18.850
and look at them what's wrong with this morning well dear the lightning 11 is playing the grant

00:13:18.850 --> 00:13:24.170
avenue tigers this morning that's right you know i almost forgot oh i wouldn't miss that for anything

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:30.000
you think beaver's team will win well the lightning 11 has recruited another man there are now seven

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.530
and strong. And Wally's giving them a secret play. So, to quote Beaver, they'll clobber

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:42.240
the creeps. Well, if we're going to the football game, I better get my dishes finished. Excuse

00:13:42.240 --> 00:13:52.160
me. Beaver, what happened? You have two black eyes. Heck, Mom, that's just stuff to keep

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:56.710
the side of my eyes. Why, sure, June. That's just a little coal black under the eyes, like

00:13:56.710 --> 00:14:00.000
the old pros use. Well, we couldn't find any coal blacks.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:01.980
- So we use some of mom's mascara.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:04.220
- Only thing is it kinda smells perfumey.

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:05.470
- Don't worry, Beave.

00:14:05.470 --> 00:14:09.580
After you get overheated, nobody will ever notice it.

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.180
- I'll tell you fellas, if you wait a little bit,

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:12.540
we'll drive you over the field.

00:14:12.540 --> 00:14:14.830
- Gee, Dad, you don't mean you're gonna come over

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:15.910
and watch me, do you?

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:19.220
- Why, of course, we wouldn't miss it.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:20.410
- Don't you want us to?

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:21.870
- Heck, Dad, that guy doesn't want

00:14:21.870 --> 00:14:23.530
his parents watching him play.

00:14:23.530 --> 00:14:25.110
It'd make me feel funny.

00:14:25.110 --> 00:14:27.260
- Why, Beaver, we're just proud of you.

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.000
- Yeah, but what if I fell down and got all bloody?

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:31.670
I didn't think of the fuss you'd make.

00:14:31.670 --> 00:14:33.330
Yeah, Mom.

00:14:33.330 --> 00:14:37.860
The guy doesn't want his mom crying and carrying on in front of people even if he was dying.

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:42.830
Well, of course, if you don't want us to, I, uh...

00:14:42.830 --> 00:14:45.400
We'll just stay here and wait for the final score.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:47.810
Well, sure, Dad. You don't have to worry about anything.

00:14:47.810 --> 00:14:50.820
I'll be there to call an ambulance or a doctor or something.

00:14:50.820 --> 00:14:53.270
Come on, Mom. So long, Dad.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:54.990
So long.

00:14:55.760 --> 00:15:00.000
Dear, how could you let them talk us out of going to the game?

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:02.380
Oh, I kind of remembered how it is.

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:06.120
Once when I was a kid, I was in a swimming meet, and my parents attended.

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:08.280
We were doing the breaststroke underwater,

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:10.660
and during the first lap, my mother jumped up and screamed,

00:15:10.860 --> 00:15:11.580
He's drowning!

00:15:13.040 --> 00:15:16.210
When I finally came up, I was so embarrassed, I wished I had.

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:17.370
Come on, let's go.

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:20.660
All right, now listen, you guys.

